DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The mmoouunnttdd utility is the server for NFS mount requests from other client
     machines.  It listens for service requests at the port indicated in the
     NFS server specification; see _N_e_t_w_o_r_k _F_i_l_e _S_y_s_t_e_m _P_r_o_t_o_c_o_l _S_p_e_c_i_f_i_c_a_t_i_o_n,
     RFC1094, Appendix A and _N_F_S_: _N_e_t_w_o_r_k _F_i_l_e _S_y_s_t_e_m _V_e_r_s_i_o_n _3 _P_r_o_t_o_c_o_l
     _S_p_e_c_i_f_i_c_a_t_i_o_n, RFC1813, Appendix I.

     The following options are available:

     --22      Allow the administrator to force clients to use only the version
             2 NFS protocol to mount file systems from this server.

     --AA      Silence the warnings related to “administrative controls”.  These
             warnings remind users that an exported “administrative control”
             directory that is not a local server file system mount point ac‐
             tually exports the entire local file system and not just the sub‐
             tree below the directory exported.  (See exports(5))

     --aa      Enforce the check for an exported directory being a file system
             mount point, if the ----aallllddiirrss option is specified in exports(5)
             for the export file line.  With this option, the exports line
             will fail, whereas without this option, a warning will be gener‐
             ated by syslog(8), but the export will be done.

     --dd      Output debugging information.  mmoouunnttdd will not detach from the
             controlling terminal and will print debugging messages to stderr.

     --ee      Ignored; included for backward compatibility.

     --hh _b_i_n_d_i_p
             Specify specific IP addresses to bind to for TCP and UDP re‐
             quests.  This option may be specified multiple times.  If no --hh
             option is specified, mmoouunnttdd will bind to INADDR_ANY.  Note that
             when specifying IP addresses with --hh, mmoouunnttdd will automatically
             add 127.0.0.1 and if IPv6 is enabled, ::1 to the list.

     --ll      Cause all succeeded mmoouunnttdd requests to be logged.

     --nn      Allow non-root mount requests to be served.  This should only be
             specified if there are clients such as PC's, that require it.  It
             will automatically clear the vfs.nfsd.nfs_privport sysctl flag,
             which controls if the kernel will accept NFS requests from re‐
             served ports only.

     --pp _p_o_r_t
             Force mmoouunnttdd to bind to the specified port, for both AF_INET and
             AF_INET6 address families.  This is typically done to ensure that
             the port which mmoouunnttdd binds to is a known quantity which can be
             used in firewall rulesets.  If mmoouunnttdd cannot bind to this port,
             an appropriate error will be recorded in the system log, and the
             daemon will then exit.

     --RR      Do not support the Mount protocol and do not register with
             rpcbind(8).  This can be done for NFSv4 only servers, since the
             Mount protocol is not used by NFSv4.  Useful for NFSv4 only
             servers that do not wish to run rpcbind(8).  showmount(8) will
             not work, however since NFSv4 mounts are not shown by
             showmount(8), this should not be an issue for an NFSv4 only
             server.

     --rr      Allow mount RPCs requests for regular files to be served.  Al‐
             though this seems to violate the mount protocol specification,
             some diskless workstations do mount requests for their swapfiles
             and expect them to be regular files.  Since a regular file cannot
             be specified in _/_e_t_c_/_e_x_p_o_r_t_s, the entire file system in which the
             swapfiles resides will have to be exported with the --aallllddiirrss
             flag.

     _e_x_p_o_r_t_s_f_i_l_e
             Specify an alternate location for the exports file.  More than
             one exports file can be specified.

     --SS      Tell mountd to suspend/resume execution of the nfsd threads when‐
             ever the exports list is being reloaded.  This avoids intermit‐
             tent access errors for clients that do NFS RPCs while the exports
             are being reloaded, but introduces a delay in RPC response while
             the reload is in progress.  If mmoouunnttdd crashes while an exports
             load is in progress, mmoouunnttdd must be restarted to get the nfsd
             threads running again, if this option is used.

     When mmoouunnttdd is started, it loads the export host addresses and options
     into the kernel using the nmount(2) system call.  After changing the ex‐
     ports file, a hangup signal should be sent to the mmoouunnttdd daemon to get it
     to reload the export information.  After sending the SIGHUP (kill -s HUP
     `cat /var/run/mountd.pid`), check the syslog output to see if mmoouunnttdd
     logged any parsing errors in the exports file.

     If multiple instances of mmoouunnttdd are being run, either in multiple jails
     or both within and outside of a jail, care must be taken to export any
     given file system in only one of the instances.  Note that the _a_l_l_o_w_._n_f_s_d
     jail parameter is required to allow mmoouunnttdd to run in a jail.  See jail(8)
     for more information.

     If mmoouunnttdd detects that the running kernel does not include NFS support,
     it will attempt to load a loadable kernel module containing NFS code, us‐
     ing kldload(2).  If this fails, or no NFS KLD was available, mmoouunnttdd exits
     with an error.  When run in a jail, the kldload(2) must be done outside
     the jail, typically by adding “nfsd” to _k_l_d___l_i_s_t in the rc.conf(5) file
     on the jail host.

FFIILLEESS
     /etc/exports         the list of exported file systems
     /var/run/mountd.pid  the pid of the currently running mountd
     /var/db/mountdtab    the current list of remote mounted file systems

